1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,480
Every few years,

2
00:00:01,520 --> 00:00:05,686
a huge stretch of the tropical
Pacific Ocean turns unusually warm,

3
00:00:05,726 --> 00:00:08,490
and weather around the
world shifts with it.

4
00:00:08,530 --> 00:00:12,888
Peruvian fishermen noticed this warm
water arriving around Christmas,

5
00:00:12,928 --> 00:00:15,222
and named it El Niño: the boy child.

6
00:00:16,022 --> 00:00:20,835
So how does El Niño form?
Let's build it, piece by piece.

7
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:25,767
Let's start with normal conditions.

8
00:00:25,807 --> 00:00:30,710
Picture the Pacific along the equator:
Indonesia and Australia on the left,

9
00:00:30,750 --> 00:00:32,530
South America on the right.

10
00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:37,938
Near the surface, the trade winds,
the steady winds of the tropics,

11
00:00:37,978 --> 00:00:41,962
blow from east to west. They
push warm surface water west,

12
00:00:42,002 --> 00:00:45,262
piling it into a deep
warm pool near Indonesia.

13
00:00:46,062 --> 00:00:51,622
Above the warm pool, moist air rises,
builds towering storm clouds, and rains.

14
00:00:51,662 --> 00:00:56,934
High up, that air flows back east and
sinks over the cool eastern Pacific,

15
00:00:56,974 --> 00:01:02,071
where skies stay dry. This giant loop
is called the Walker circulation.

16
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:07,841
Now let's look beneath the surface.

17
00:01:07,881 --> 00:01:11,582
A warm upper layer of ocean
floats on cold, deep water.

18
00:01:11,622 --> 00:01:14,644
The boundary between
them is the thermocline:

19
00:01:14,684 --> 00:01:18,425
a thin zone where temperature
drops quickly with depth.

20
00:01:19,226 --> 00:01:25,072
Because the winds pile warm water in the
west, the thermocline there sits deep,

21
00:01:25,112 --> 00:01:30,436
about 150 meters down. In the east,
it's only a few tens of meters deep.

22
00:01:30,476 --> 00:01:32,339
So the thermocline tilts.

23
00:01:33,139 --> 00:01:37,089
Off South America, the winds,
helped by Earth's rotation,

24
00:01:37,129 --> 00:01:40,938
push surface water away from
the coast and the equator.

25
00:01:40,978 --> 00:01:45,208
Cold water rises from below to
replace it. This is upwelling.

26
00:01:45,248 --> 00:01:46,888
It feeds rich fisheries,

27
00:01:46,928 --> 00:01:52,177
and creates a strip of cool water along
the equator called the cold tongue.

28
00:01:55,500 --> 00:02:00,211
These pieces reinforce one another, in
what's called the Bjerknes feedback,

29
00:02:00,251 --> 00:02:02,722
after the meteorologist Jacob Bjerknes.

30
00:02:03,522 --> 00:02:07,590
Strong trade winds drive upwelling,
which keeps the east cold.

31
00:02:07,630 --> 00:02:12,824
A cold east and a warm west make a big
temperature difference across the ocean.

32
00:02:12,864 --> 00:02:16,203
That difference creates an
air-pressure difference,

33
00:02:16,243 --> 00:02:18,919
which drives the trade winds even harder.

34
00:02:18,959 --> 00:02:23,398
Stronger winds mean more upwelling,
and the loop goes around again.

35
00:02:24,198 --> 00:02:27,383
A loop like this can
hold the Pacific steady.

36
00:02:27,423 --> 00:02:29,644
But it can also run in reverse.

37
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:34,395
That's how an El Niño begins.

38
00:02:34,435 --> 00:02:38,933
The trade winds weaken, sometimes
helped by bursts of westerly wind,

39
00:02:38,973 --> 00:02:41,842
which blow from the
west for days or weeks.

40
00:02:42,642 --> 00:02:47,499
With less wind holding it back, the
piled-up warm water sloshes east.

41
00:02:47,539 --> 00:02:53,034
It travels as a Kelvin wave: a broad,
slow bulge that rides along the equator,

42
00:02:53,074 --> 00:02:56,369
pushing the thermocline
down by tens of meters.

43
00:02:56,409 --> 00:02:59,461
It crosses the Pacific
in about two months.

44
00:03:00,261 --> 00:03:04,248
In the east, the thermocline
deepens and the tilt flattens.

45
00:03:04,288 --> 00:03:09,476
Upwelling now brings up warm water
instead of cold, so the surface heats up.

46
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:16,128
Now the Bjerknes feedback runs in reverse.

47
00:03:16,168 --> 00:03:19,496
A warmer east means a smaller
temperature difference,

48
00:03:19,536 --> 00:03:24,428
which weakens the winds further, which
lets even more warm water spread east.

49
00:03:25,228 --> 00:03:29,527
Warm water now covers much of
the central and eastern Pacific.

50
00:03:29,567 --> 00:03:34,285
The rising air and heavy rain follow
it, moving away from Indonesia.

51
00:03:34,325 --> 00:03:37,614
The Walker circulation
weakens and shifts east.

52
00:03:38,414 --> 00:03:43,350
It even disturbs the jet stream,
a fast river of air high above us.

53
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:47,212
In winter, the jet often
strengthens and dips south,

54
00:03:47,252 --> 00:03:50,109
steering storms toward the southern US.

55
00:03:50,149 --> 00:03:53,937
El Niño usually peaks
between November and January.

56
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:00,913
These changes ripple around the
world through teleconnections:

57
00:04:00,953 --> 00:04:03,180
links between weather in faraway places.

58
00:04:03,980 --> 00:04:09,667
El Niño often brings drought to
Indonesia, Australia, and southern Africa,

59
00:04:09,707 --> 00:04:11,989
and a weaker monsoon to India.

60
00:04:12,029 --> 00:04:15,626
Peru and Ecuador can see
heavy rain and floods.

61
00:04:15,666 --> 00:04:19,186
The southern US tends
to have a wetter winter,

62
00:04:19,226 --> 00:04:23,056
while Canada and the
northern US are often milder.

63
00:04:23,096 --> 00:04:26,383
Atlantic hurricanes
are usually suppressed,

64
00:04:26,423 --> 00:04:30,912
while more storms form in the
central and eastern Pacific.

65
00:04:31,712 --> 00:04:33,831
As the ocean releases heat,

66
00:04:33,871 --> 00:04:40,149
the global average temperature often
rises by about 0.1 to 0.2 degrees Celsius.

67
00:04:40,189 --> 00:04:43,428
But these are tendencies, not guarantees.

68
00:04:43,468 --> 00:04:45,627
Every El Niño is different.

69
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:49,557
El Niño doesn't last forever.

70
00:04:49,597 --> 00:04:53,967
While Kelvin waves carry warm
water east, slower ocean waves,

71
00:04:54,007 --> 00:04:56,352
called Rossby waves, travel west.

72
00:04:56,392 --> 00:05:00,979
They bounce off the western edge
of the Pacific and return east,

73
00:05:01,019 --> 00:05:03,437
gradually lifting the thermocline.

74
00:05:03,477 --> 00:05:07,309
Meanwhile, El Niño drains
heat away from the equator.

75
00:05:08,109 --> 00:05:12,344
The trade winds recover, upwelling
returns, and the east cools.

76
00:05:12,384 --> 00:05:16,959
Sometimes the system overshoots
into La Niña, El Niño's counterpart:

77
00:05:16,999 --> 00:05:20,759
stronger trade winds, an
unusually cold eastern Pacific,

78
00:05:20,799 --> 00:05:24,803
and weather that is often
roughly the reverse of El Niño's.

79
00:05:27,500 --> 00:05:32,578
Together, El Niño and La Niña form
ENSO, the El Niño–Southern Oscillation.

80
00:05:32,618 --> 00:05:35,344
The Southern Oscillation is the matching

81
00:05:35,384 --> 00:05:38,358
see-saw in air pressure
across the Pacific.

82
00:05:39,158 --> 00:05:41,391
It's a natural, irregular cycle,

83
00:05:41,431 --> 00:05:45,227
and corals show it has existed
for thousands of years.

84
00:05:45,267 --> 00:05:48,282
El Niño returns roughly
every 2 to 7 years,

85
00:05:48,322 --> 00:05:51,874
and each event usually
lasts about 9 to 12 months.

86
00:05:52,674 --> 00:05:55,341
Climate change didn't create El Niño.

87
00:05:55,381 --> 00:06:00,316
But on a warmer planet, its impacts,
like heat waves and heavy rain,

88
00:06:00,356 --> 00:06:05,478
can hit harder. How ENSO itself will
change is still being researched.

89
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:13,963
So, to recap: the trade winds weaken,
warm water sloshes east, the rain follows,

90
00:06:14,003 --> 00:06:16,067
and the whole planet feels it.

91
00:06:16,867 --> 00:06:19,142
Next time you hear that
El Niño is on the way,

92
00:06:19,182 --> 00:06:21,598
you'll know what's happening
out in the Pacific.
